title: Rattle Structure

Generate new systems from the current one by perturbing its geometry: either by moving
every atom a fixed distance in a random direction, or by running a constant-temperature
molecular dynamics with a force field and collecting snapshots from the trajectory. The
new structures are added to the tree as a group of new systems, for instance to prepare
distorted configurations or to check the stability of a structure.

# Options
- Method: "Random displacements" moves every atom by the same distance in a random
  direction, different for each atom. "Molecular dynamics" runs an NVT simulation on the
  system and extracts snapshots from it.
- Structures (random displacements): the number of structures to generate.
- Displacement (Å): the distance every atom is moved.
- Force field and Potential (molecular dynamics): the method for energies and forces, and
  the embedded-atom potential file when EAM is chosen. Only the methods that apply to the
  system are listed.
- Temperature (K): the temperature of the NVT dynamics.
- Time step (fs): the integration time step; its value in atomic units is shown next to
  it.
- Equilibration: the number of steps run before the first snapshot is taken.
- Snapshots: the number of snapshots collected.
- Stride: the number of steps between consecutive snapshots. The total number of steps is
  shown below.
- Estimate Cost: run a few steps of the dynamics, discard them, and report how long the
  whole run would take and the time per step.
- Close after generating: close the window once the structures have been generated.
- Generate starts the run; Stop ends the dynamics early and keeps the snapshots collected
  so far.

# Results
During a dynamics run the window reports the equilibration step and the number of
snapshots collected. The generated structures appear in the tree as a group of new
systems and can be opened in the view like any other.

# Notes
- The dynamics settings are shared with the Dynamics window of the same system.
- A run on a large system can take a while; use "Estimate Cost" first.
